= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**No, $[0,1]$ is not <open>.** Every basic <neighbourhood> $[a,b)$ of $1$ has $b>1$, so it contains points larger than $1$. Therefore $1$ has no <open> <neighbourhood> contained in $[0,1]$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The <interval> $[0,1)$ is a basic <open set> contained in $[0,1]$. The remaining point $1$ is not an <interior> point, by the preceding argument. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{int}_{\mathcal T}[0,1]=[0,1).}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**The only <isolated point> of the <subset> $[0,1]$ is $1$.** Indeed,
$$
[1,2)\cap[0,1]=\{1\},
$$
so $\{1\}$ is <open> in the <subspace topology>. If $0\leq x<1$, every basic <neighbourhood> $[a,b)$ containing $x$ also contains a point strictly between $x$ and $\min(b,1)$, hence another point of $[0,1]$. Such an $x$ is not an <isolated point> of the <subset>. Here isolation refers to $[0,1]$ with its <subspace topology>; $1$ is not an <isolated point> of the ambient <Sorgenfrey line>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Let $U$ be <open> in the <Euclidean topology> on the <real line>, and let $x\in U$. There is an $\varepsilon>0$ with $(x-\varepsilon,x+\varepsilon)\subseteq U$. The basic <open set> $[x,x+\varepsilon)$ contains $x$ and is contained in $U$. Taking the <union> of these basic <neighbourhoods> over $x\in U$ shows that $U\in\mathcal T$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T_{\mathrm{usual}}\subseteq\mathcal T.}
$$
In fact this is a strictly <finer topology>: $[0,1)$ is <open> in $\mathcal T$ but not in the <Euclidean topology>, since no usual <open ball> about $0$ is contained in it.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

The usual <real line> is <connected>. In contrast,
$$
\mathbb R_{\mathcal T}=(-\infty,0)\sqcup[0,\infty)
$$
is a <separation of a topological space>. Both pieces are nonempty and disjoint; the first is <open> by part (c), and the second is the <open> <union> $\bigcup_{n\geq1}[0,n)$. Thus the <Sorgenfrey line> is not <connected>. Since a <homeomorphism> preserves <connectedness>, **the two <topological spaces> are not <homeomorphic>.** Merely showing that their <topologies> differ would not by itself exclude some other <homeomorphism> between them.

