{c}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**$X$ is not <compact>.** In the <subspace topology> on $X=[0,1]$, consider the <open cover>
$$
\mathcal U=\{[0,1-1/n):n\geq2\}\cup\{\{1\}\}.
$$
The <intervals> are basic <open sets> in the ambient <Sorgenfrey line>, and $\{1\}=X\cap[1,2)$ is <open> in $X$. Every $x<1$ belongs to one of those <intervals>, while the <singleton> covers $1$.

A <finite subcover> would have to include $\{1\}$. Among its finitely many other members, let $N$ be the largest index. Their <union> is $[0,1-1/N)$, leaving $1-1/N$ uncovered. Selecting no such member also fails to cover $0$. Hence no <finite subcover> exists.

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**$X$ does not have <sequential compactness>.** Take the <sequence>
$$
x_n=1-\frac1n\qquad(n\geq1).
$$
Every <subsequence> has usual <limit of a sequence> $1$. If a <subsequence> converged in the <subspace topology> of the <Sorgenfrey line>, it would also converge in the usual <subspace topology>, since part (c) makes the <identity function> from the former to the latter <continuous>. Uniqueness of a <limit of a sequence> in the usual <Hausdorff space> therefore forces that <limit of a sequence> to be $1$.

But $\{1\}$ is an <open> <neighbourhood> of $1$ in $X$, and no term $x_n$ belongs to it. Thus no <subsequence> converges to $1$ in $X$, proving the assertion.

