Use the <Heaviside step function> with the value at the jump chosen as follows:
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\begin{cases}0,&x<0,\\1,&x\geq0.\end{cases}}
$$
For any <open set> in the target <Sorgenfrey line>, its <preimage> is one of $\varnothing$, $\mathbb R$, $(-\infty,0)$, and $[0,\infty)$. All four are <open> in the source <Sorgenfrey line>, so $f$ is <continuous>. In the <Euclidean topology>, the <preimage> of $(1/2,3/2)$ is $[0,\infty)$, which is not <open>. Hence the same <function> is not <continuous> as a usual real-to-real <map>.

= ii
{parent=f}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Take
$$
\boxed{g(x)=-x.}
$$
It is <continuous> for the <Euclidean metric>, since $|g(x)-g(y)|=|x-y|$. But the basic <open set> $[0,1)$ of the target <Sorgenfrey line> has <preimage>
$$
g^{-1}([0,1))=(-1,0].
$$
This is not <open> in the source <Sorgenfrey line>: every basic <neighbourhood> of $0$ contains positive points. Thus $g$ is not <continuous> for the <lower limit topology>.

