= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <unit circle> is <Hausdorff> and <second countable>, since both properties pass to <topological subspaces> of the <Euclidean plane>. To exhibit its local <coordinate charts>, consider the four <open> <semicircles>
$$
U_{y,+}=S^1\cap\{y>0\},\quad U_{y,-}=S^1\cap\{y<0\},\quad U_{x,+}=S^1\cap\{x>0\},\quad U_{x,-}=S^1\cap\{x<0\}.
$$
They cover $S^1$. On $U_{y,\pm}$, the <projection map> $(x,y)\mapsto x$ is a <homeomorphism> onto the <open interval> $(-1,1)$, with <continuous> <inverse function>
$$
t\longmapsto(t,\pm\sqrt{1-t^2}).
$$
On $U_{x,\pm}$, use $(x,y)\mapsto y$, with <inverse function> $t\mapsto(\pm\sqrt{1-t^2},t)$. These <coordinate charts> show that **$S^1$ is a one-dimensional <topological manifold> with empty <manifold boundary>.**

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $o=(0,0)$ be the common point of the two <circles>. For sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$, the <open> <neighbourhood>
$$
W=X\cap B_\varepsilon(o)
$$
has the following local structure: $W\setminus\{o\}$ consists of four disjoint <open arcs>, an upper and a lower branch from each <circle>. These are its four <connected components>, each <open> and <closed> relative to $W\setminus\{o\}$.

Suppose a <coordinate chart> of some <dimension of a manifold> $n$ existed at $o$. The case $n=0$ would make $o$ an <isolated point>, which it is not. For $n\geq1$, shrink the chart inside $W$ so that its <image> is an <open ball> centred at the <image> of $o$. Its <domain of a function> $V$ is an <open> <neighbourhood> of $o$, so it meets all four arms of $W$. Intersecting those arms with $V\setminus\{o\}$ gives four disjoint nonempty <clopen sets> partitioning $V\setminus\{o\}$; hence there are at least four <connected components>.

But a <punctured ball> in $\mathbb R$ has exactly two <connected components>, and a <punctured ball> in $\mathbb R^n$ for $n\geq2$ is <path connected>. A <homeomorphism> preserves <connected components>, giving a contradiction in every <dimension of a manifold>. Thus **$X$ is not a <topological manifold>**. The argument uses arbitrarily small <coordinate charts>; the fact that the whole $X\setminus\{o\}$ has two pieces alone would not be a local obstruction.

