Label the <hexagon> <polygon vertices> $v_0,\ldots,v_5$ in the direction in which its boundary word is $abca^{-1}b^{-1}c^{-1}$, with the first side running from $v_0$ to $v_1$. Matching the endpoints according to the arrows gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
a:&\quad v_0\sim v_4,\quad v_1\sim v_3,\\
b:&\quad v_1\sim v_5,\quad v_2\sim v_4,\\
c:&\quad v_2\sim v_0,\quad v_3\sim v_5.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the two vertex <equivalence classes> are $\{v_0,v_2,v_4\}$ and $\{v_1,v_3,v_5\}$.

The <polygonal schema> gives a <compact> <connected> <surface> with empty <manifold boundary>. Interior points have <disc> <neighbourhoods>; a paired <polygon edge> glues two half-<disc> <neighbourhoods> into a <disc>. At each of the two vertex <equivalence classes>, its three corner sectors join cyclically, so the <vertex link in a polygonal schema> is a <circle>, again giving a <disc> <neighbourhood>. Pairing every label once positively and once negatively makes these gluings consistent with an <orientation>, so the resulting <surface> is <orientable>.

The resulting <CW complex> has <CW cells> comprising two vertices, three edges and one face. Its <Euler characteristic> is
$$
\chi=V-E+F=2-3+1=0.
$$
By the <classification of compact surfaces>, a <connected> <closed orientable surface> with $\chi=0$ has <genus> $g$ satisfying $2-2g=0$, hence $g=1$. **The <quotient space> is a <torus>.**

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**True.** Label the <pentagon> <polygon vertices> clockwise from the upper left as
$$
v_0=(0,2),\quad v_1=(2,2),\quad v_2=(2,0),\quad v_3=(1,0),\quad v_4=(0,1).
$$
Use $a$ for the upward single arrows and $b$ for the rightward double arrows in the original diagram. Their endpoint identifications are
$$
\begin{aligned}
a:&\quad v_4\sim v_2,\quad v_0\sim v_1,\\
b:&\quad v_0\sim v_3,\quad v_1\sim v_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
All five <polygon vertices> are therefore identified. The remaining diagonal <polygon edge>, from $v_3$ to $v_4$, has its endpoints identified and is the only <boundary component of a surface>.

The paired <polygon edges> join half-<disc> <neighbourhoods> into <disc> <neighbourhoods>. At the common vertex, the five corner sectors join in one chain whose two free ends lie on the unpaired <polygon edge>: its <vertex link in a polygonal schema> is a <closed interval>. Hence that point has a half-<disc> <neighbourhood>. This verifies that the <quotient space> is a <surface with boundary>, rather than merely counting its <CW cells>. The paired <polygon edges> are oppositely traversed around the <polygon>, so this <surface> is <orientable>.

Its <CW cells> comprise one vertex, three edges (the $a$ pair, the $b$ pair, and the free <polygon edge>), and one face. Therefore
$$
\chi=1-3+1=-1,\qquad b=1,\qquad 2-2g-b=-1\Longrightarrow g=1.
$$
The <classification of compact surfaces> identifies it as **a <torus> with an <open disc> removed**.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**False: the result is again a <torus> with one <open disc> removed.** Label the <hexagon> <polygon vertices> clockwise from the upper left as
$$
v_0=(0,2),\quad v_1=(1,2),\quad v_2=(2,1),\quad v_3=(2,0),\quad v_4=(1,0),\quad v_5=(0,1).
$$
With the same arrow labels as in part (ii),
$$
\begin{aligned}
a:&\quad v_5\sim v_3,\quad v_0\sim v_2,\\
b:&\quad v_0\sim v_4,\quad v_1\sim v_3.
\end{aligned}
$$
There are two vertex <equivalence classes>, $P=\{v_0,v_2,v_4\}$ and $Q=\{v_1,v_3,v_5\}$. The free diagonal <polygon edges> are $v_1v_2$ and $v_4v_5$. Each joins $P$ to $Q$, so together they form **one <boundary component of a surface>, not two**. At each vertex <equivalence class> the three corner sectors join in a single chain between these two free <polygon edges>. Thus each <vertex link in a polygonal schema> is a <closed interval>, and the <quotient space> is an <orientable> <surface with boundary>.

Its <CW complex> has <CW cells> comprising two vertices, four edges and one face, giving
$$
\chi=2-4+1=-1,\qquad b=1,\qquad g=1.
$$
In contrast, a <torus> with two disjoint <open discs> removed has two <boundary components of a surface> and <Euler characteristic> $2-2-2=-2$. Either <topological invariant> rules out the proposed <homeomorphism>.

\Image[2021-polygon-boundaries.png]
{title=After identifying equally labelled arrows, both polygons have one boundary circle; in the hexagon the two free edges join the two vertex classes}

